<%- include('../partials/header', { title }) %>
  <%# ---- THE CHILD'S RESULT, IN THE ORDER A PARENT READS IT (school's request, 2026-09-17) ----
      “The result section does not look good — make it easy to understand, and give me a
      download button so the result and the graph go into one PDF.”

      One panel answers one question, and the circles come first because a circle is read
      faster than a column of numbers:

        1. HOW IS MY CHILD DOING?   — one big circle (average + grade) and three chips
                                      (best subject, the one to work on, terms entered).
                                      There used to be two extra cards here repeating the very
                                      same 75% and B+ — the same number twice makes a parent
                                      wonder which one is right, so they are gone.
        2. WHERE, SUBJECT BY SUBJECT? — one circle per subject, best first (§ ANALYSIS).
        3. IS HE GOING UP?          — the terms, each with an ▲ / ▼ against the term before.
        4. WHAT DOES IT LOOK LIKE?  — the bars, drawn as SVG so they PRINT (§ chart partial).
        5. THE PAPER ITSELF         — the marks table, exactly as the school entered it.
      Every number comes from services/analysis.js. Nothing on this page is worked out here. ---- %>
  <%# ---- THE CHILD'S OWN CARD, exactly the one on the home tab (school's request, 2026-09-17):
      “make the result section like this card — a download icon where the bell is.”

      Same card, same photo (or the initials when the office has not added one), same class /
      Roll No. chips — a parent recognises their child's card on every screen. ONE thing differs:
      the round button in the corner is the DOWNLOAD (it prints this page, and the print rules turn
      it into a one-page result card) instead of the bell that opens the inbox.

      The card carries `no-print`: on paper the letter head below already says whose result this is,
      and the sheet has to stay ONE page. The button keeps the classes the tests and the print rules
      know — `btn btn-primary pg-pdf no-print` — and `data-confirm="off"` keeps the “are you sure?”
      dialog away from a download. %>
  <div class="kid-card pg-kid-card no-print">
    <% if (child.photo) { %>
      <img src="<%= child.photo %>" alt="<%= child.full_name %>" class="kid-photo">
    <% } else { %>
      <span class="kid-photo kid-photo-ph"><%= child.full_name.split(' ').map(w => w[0]).join('').slice(0, 2).toUpperCase() %></span>
    <% } %>
    <div class="kid-info">
      <span class="kid-name-row">
        <b><%= child.full_name %></b>
        <%# the 📈 lives HERE (2026-09-18): the school asked for the graph icon on the
            result card, right after the name — it scrolls to the subject circles below
            (the same page's diagram), instead of leaving for another page. %>
        <a href="#pg-subjects" class="kid-perf-btn"
           title="See subject graphs" aria-label="See subject graphs">📈</a>
      </span>
      <span class="kid-meta">
        <span class="badge blue"><%= child.class_name %><%= child.section ? ' ' + child.section : '' %></span>
        <% if (child.roll_no) { %><span class="kid-roll">Roll No. <%= child.roll_no %></span><% } %>
      </span>
      <% if (!child.photo) { %><small class="kid-hint">📷 The school office will add the photo</small><% } %>
    </div>
    <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary pg-pdf no-print" data-confirm="off"
            onclick="window.print()" title="Download the result — on the print screen choose “Save as PDF”"
            aria-label="Download the whole result as a PDF">⬇️ Result PDF</button>
  </div>

  <%# on paper the picker is noise: the printed card is about ONE child %>
  <div class="no-print"><%- include('../partials/child_picker', { children, childId }) %></div>

  <%# the letter head that only exists on paper, so a shared PDF says whose result it is %>
  <div class="print-only print-head pg-print-head">
    <div>
      <b><%= school.schoolName %></b>
      <span class="pg-print-sub"><%= child.class_name %><%= child.section ? ' ' + child.section : '' %> · Roll No. <%= child.roll_no %></span>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-print-right">
      <b><%= child.full_name %></b>
      <span class="pg-print-sub">Result card · printed <%= fmtDate(new Date()) %></span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# ---- 1. HOW IS MY CHILD DOING? -------------------------------------------------- %>
  <div class="panel pg-glance">
    <div class="panel-head">
      <h3>🎯 Result at a glance</h3>
      <span class="panel-head-note">
        <% if (result.hasMarks) { %><%= result.totals.subjects %> subject<%= result.totals.subjects === 1 ? '' : 's' %> · <%= fmtDate(new Date()) %><% } else { %>waiting for the first result<% } %>
      </span>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-body">
      <%# the one plain sentence, above the circle — it reads the same numbers as everything else %>
      <p class="result-line"><%= result.line %></p>

      <%- include('../partials/result_rings', {
        big: result.overall,
        legend: false,
        empty: 'No marks have been entered yet — the diagram fills in as the school adds results.',
      }) %>

      <% if (result.hasMarks) { %>
        <%# school's request, 2026-09-18: the “Best subject / Work on this” fact chips are
            GONE here too — the subject circles right below already show every subject with
            its percentage, sorted best first; saying the best one twice is noise.
            Terms-entered and marks-counted stay: they are facts the circles do not show. %>
        <div class="pg-facts">
          <div class="pg-fact">
            <span class="pg-fact-ico" aria-hidden="true">📅</span>
            <span class="pg-fact-body">
              <span class="pg-fact-label">Terms entered</span>
              <b><%= result.facts.termsEntered %> of <%= result.facts.termsTotal %></b>
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="pg-fact">
            <span class="pg-fact-ico" aria-hidden="true">🧮</span>
            <span class="pg-fact-body">
              <span class="pg-fact-label">Marks counted</span>
              <b><%= result.facts.obtained %> of <%= result.facts.max %></b>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
      <% } %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# ---- 2. SUBJECT BY SUBJECT ------------------------------------------------------ %>
  <div class="panel pg-subjects">
    <div class="panel-head">
      <h3>📚 Subject by subject</h3>
      <span class="panel-head-note">
        <% if (result.hasMarks) { %>best first · a written paper and its oral are one subject<% } else { %>nothing entered yet<% } %>
      </span>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-body">
      <%- include('../partials/result_rings', {
        rings: result.subjects,
        legend: true,
        empty: 'No subject has marks yet.',
      }) %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# ---- 3. IS HE GOING UP? -------------------------------------------------------- %>
  <div class="panel pg-terms">
    <div class="panel-head">
      <h3>📅 Term by term</h3>
      <span class="panel-head-note">
        <% if (result.hasMarks) { %>▲ up · ▼ down, against the term before<% } else { %>nothing entered yet<% } %>
      </span>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-body">
      <%- include('../partials/result_rings', {
        strip: result.terms,
        empty: 'No term has been entered yet.',
      }) %>
      <% if (result.hasMarks && result.totals.entries < result.totals.subjects * 3) { %>
        <div class="result-more">
          ℹ️ <%= result.terms.filter(t => t.value === null).length %> of <%= result.terms.length %> term<%= result.terms.length === 1 ? '' : 's' %>
          not entered yet — the averages will move as the school adds the later terms.
        </div>
      <% } %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# ---- 4. WHAT DOES IT LOOK LIKE? ------------------------------------------------ %>
  <%- include('../partials/performance_chart', { chart: result.chart }) %>

  <%# ---- 5. THE PAPER ITSELF ------------------------------------------------------- %>
  <div class="panel pg-table">
    <div class="panel-head">
      <h3>📊 Marks table</h3>
      <span class="panel-head-note">exactly as the school entered it</span>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-body no-pad">
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Term 1</th><th>Term 2</th><th>Term 3</th><th class="text-right">Average</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <% marks.forEach(m => { %>
            <tr>
              <td><b><%= m.subject %></b></td>
              <td><%= m['Term 1'] ? m['Term 1'].obtained + '/' + m['Term 1'].max : '—' %></td>
              <td><%= m['Term 2'] ? m['Term 2'].obtained + '/' + m['Term 2'].max : '—' %></td>
              <td><%= m['Term 3'] ? m['Term 3'].obtained + '/' + m['Term 3'].max : '—' %></td>
              <td class="text-right">
                <b><%= typeof m.avgText === 'string' ? m.avgText : m.avg + '%' %></b>
                <% if (m.tone && m.tone !== 'none') { %>
                  <span class="pg-band tone-<%= m.tone %>"><%= m.word %></span>
                <% } %>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <% }) %>
            <% if (!marks.length) { %>
            <tr><td colspan="5" class="empty">No marks have been entered yet. They appear here after the school publishes the result.</td></tr>
            <% } %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <% if (marks.length) { %>
        <%# SCREEN-ONLY note (school's request, 2026-09-18): the long “how the numbers add up”
            sentence is help for the person READING the screen; on the downloaded PDF it was
            just the “content the whole result…” line the school asked to remove. %>
        <div class="table-foot-note no-print">
          A written paper and its <b>oral</b> are added together in the circles and the graph above (Maths 77 + 4 of 100),
          because that is how the report card reads. This table lists every paper exactly as the school entered it.
        </div>
      <% } %>
    </div>
  </div>
<%# ---- THE ONE-PAGE FIT (school's request, 2026-09-17) --------------------------------------
    “the result and the graph should download as ONE PDF page.” The print rules in style.css
    already trim the sheet, and the whole result of an ordinary child fits. This is the safety
    valve for the heavy ones (a class with 19 papers x 3 terms): a moment before the browser
    lays the sheet out, measure the printed page and shrink it by just enough. Nothing happens
    on the screen, nothing is fetched, and with JavaScript switched off the worst case is one
    extra sheet — never a missing section. Kept here, not in /js, so the page is still
    self-contained when it is saved and printed offline.
    Verified with: node .freebuff/print-check.js (a real headless browser counts the sheets). -‐ %>
<script>
(function () {
  var PAPER = 1069;   // A4 (297mm) minus the two 7mm margins, at 96dpi
  var FLOOR = 0.62;   // never shrink past this: a PDF nobody can read is not a result
  var main = document.querySelector('.main') || document.body;
  function fit() {
    main.style.zoom = '';
    var h = main.scrollHeight;
    if (h > PAPER) main.style.zoom = String(Math.max(FLOOR, PAPER / h));
  }
  window.addEventListener('beforeprint', fit);
  window.addEventListener('afterprint', function () { main.style.zoom = ''; });
})();
</script>
<%- include('../partials/footer') %>
